- id: 91
  question: "Which field in a Pod spec specifies the DNS policy used by the pod?"
  options:
    A: "dnsPolicy"
    B: "dnsConfig"
    C: "dnsPolicyType"
    D: "dnsMode"
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "dnsPolicy sets the DNS policy (ClusterFirst, Default, etc.) for the pod. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/dns-pod-service/#dns-policy)"
    B: "dnsConfig provides additional DNS settings but does not select the policy."
    C: "dnsPolicyType is not a Kubernetes field."
    D: "dnsMode does not exist."

- id: 92
  question: "What does setting 'spec.restartPolicy: OnFailure' in a Job do?"
  options:
    A: "Restarts the pod indefinitely regardless of exit code"
    B: "Restarts the pod only when it exits with a non‑zero status, up to backoffLimit times"
    C: "Prevents any restarts, causing the job to fail on first error"
    D: "Makes the job run continuously like a Deployment"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "OnFailure restarts only on failure, not indefinitely."
    B: "OnFailure restarts failed pods, respecting backoffLimit. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/job/#restart-policy)"
    C: "Never would prevent restarts."
    D: "Jobs are finite; this describes a Deployment."

- id: 93
  question: "Which annotation can be used to enable sidecar injection by Istio?"
  options:
    A: "sidecar.istio.io/inject: \"true\""
    B: "istio.io/sidecar-inject: \"yes\""
    C: "inject.istio.io/sidecar: \"enabled\""
    D: "istio.sidecar/inject: \"true\""
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "The standard annotation is sidecar.istio.io/inject. (https://istio.io/latest/docs/setup/additional-setup/sidecar-injection/#automatic-sidecar-injection)"
    B: "Incorrect namespace."
    C: "Wrong annotation format."
    D: "Incorrect ordering of keys."

- id: 94
  question: "What is the effect of setting 'spec.podManagementPolicy: Parallel' in a StatefulSet?"
  options:
    A: "Pods are created sequentially, one after another"
    B: "All Pods are created simultaneously, speeding up startup"
    C: "Pods are never deleted automatically"
    D: "The StatefulSet behaves like a Deployment"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "Parallel does the opposite of OrderedReady."
    B: "Parallel creates all replicas at once. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/#pod-management-policies)"
    C: "Deletion behavior is unchanged."
    D: "StatefulSet semantics remain."

- id: 95
  question: "Which field in a Service definition determines whether the Service is headless?"
  options:
    A: "clusterIP: \"None\""
    B: "type: Headless"
    C: "headless: true"
    D: "externalName: \"none\""
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "Setting clusterIP to \"None\" creates a headless Service. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#headless-services)"
    B: "There is no 'Headless' type."
    C: "No such field."
    D: "ExternalName is a different Service type."

- id: 96
  question: "What does the 'kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath=\"{.items[*].status.capacity.cpu}\"' command output?"
  options:
    A: "A list of node names"
    B: "The total CPU capacity of each node in cores"
    C: "The current CPU usage of each node"
    D: "The pod count per node"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "Node names are under .metadata.name."
    B: "The jsonpath extracts the CPU capacity field."
    C: "Usage requires Metrics Server."
    D: "Pod count is not part of capacity."

- id: 97
  question: "Which Kubernetes object is used to define a set of rules that restrict which pods can communicate with each other?"
  options:
    A: "NetworkPolicy"
    B: "PodSecurityPolicy"
    C: "ResourceQuota"
    D: "Ingress"
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "NetworkPolicy defines allowed ingress/egress traffic between pods. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/)"
    B: "PodSecurityPolicy deals with security contexts."
    C: "ResourceQuota limits resource consumption."
    D: "Ingress manages external HTTP traffic."

- id: 98
  question: "What is the purpose of the 'spec.tolerations' field in a pod spec?"
  options:
    A: "Define node affinity rules"
    B: "Allow pods to be scheduled onto nodes with matching taints"
    C: "Set resource limits for the pod"
    D: "Configure the pod's DNS policy"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "Affinity is separate."
    B: "Tolerations let pods tolerate taints. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/taint-and-toleration/)"
    C: "Resources are defined under resources."
    D: "dnsPolicy handles DNS."

- id: 99
  question: "Which field in a Deployment's pod template can be used to set environment variables from a ConfigMap?"
  options:
    A: "envFrom"
    B: "env"
    C: "configMapRef"
    D: "volumeMounts"
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "envFrom imports all key‑value pairs from a ConfigMap as env vars. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/environment-variable-expose-pod-information/#use-configmap-as-environment-variables)"
    B: "env can reference individual keys but not bulk import."
    C: "configMapRef is not a top‑level field."
    D: "volumeMounts mount files, not env vars."

- id: 100
  question: "What does setting 'spec.type: LoadBalancer' on a Service do in a cloud‑native environment?"
  options:
    A: "Creates a NodePort on each node"
    B: "Allocates an external IP via the cloud provider's load‑balancer service"
    C: "Exposes the Service only within the cluster"
    D: "Disables all traffic to the Service"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "NodePort is a different type."
    B: "LoadBalancer triggers cloud provider provisioning of an external LB. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#loadbalancer)"
    C: "ClusterIP would keep it internal."
    D: "LoadBalancer exposes, not disables."
